        <h2>Where a Copied Picture Actually Goes on macOS</h2>

        <p>This is the part most guides skip, and it explains a problem a lot of people run into without knowing why: macOS's clipboard, the pasteboard, stores images primarily as TIFF, not as PNG or JPEG. When you copy a picture, whatever app you copied it from typically hands the system a TIFF representation, and it is up to the app you paste into to ask for something else if it wants a different format. Microsoft Office apps, for instance, paste images as TIFF by default for exactly this reason. TIFF is usually uncompressed or only lightly compressed, so a picture that would be a few hundred kilobytes as a PNG can sit on your clipboard as several times that in TIFF form.</p>

        <p>That matters the moment you try to save it. If you paste into an app that just writes out whatever the clipboard gives it, you get a needlessly large file without ever choosing that outcome. It is also why a browser-based paste tool behaves differently, covered below.</p>

        <h2>Turning a Copied Picture Into a File: Three Real Methods</h2>

        <h3>Method 1: Preview's New from Clipboard</h3>

        <p>Open Preview, then <strong>File &gt; New from Clipboard</strong> (or press Cmd+N after opening Preview from Spotlight). The copied picture opens as a new, untitled image. From there, use <strong>File &gt; Export</strong>, not Save, so you get the format dropdown: choose PNG, JPEG, or another format explicitly, rather than letting Preview keep whatever representation it received. If you just press Cmd+S without checking, you may still get a format picker in the save panel, but it is easy to click through without noticing, and the difference between "PNG" and whatever the pasteboard handed over can be a large one for no visible reason in the resulting file. One more thing worth knowing: the untitled image Preview creates is not saved anywhere until you explicitly save or export it, so closing the window without doing so loses it. If New from Clipboard appears greyed out, it usually means nothing image-shaped is currently on the clipboard, often because the copy did not actually succeed or something else was copied afterward.</p>

        <h3>Method 2: Paste into Pages, Notes, or Mail</h3>

        <p>Dropping the image into a document and exporting or saving from there works, but it carries baggage: Pages and Notes attach the image inside their own document format, and getting a clean standalone file back out means exporting again and stripping formatting. It is a reasonable option if you are already in one of those apps for another reason, not a good first choice.</p>

        <h3>Method 3: Paste Into a Browser Tool</h3>

        <p>This is where the TIFF issue above stops being a problem rather than something to route around. The Clipboard API that browsers use to read pasted content is required to support PNG, and Chromium-based browsers and Safari 13.1+ implement it that way: when you paste into a web page, the browser normalizes whatever the system clipboard held into a PNG before your page ever sees it. Practically, that means a tool like <a href="https://pastedownloadapp.com/">pastedownloadapp.com</a> never has to deal with a bloated TIFF at all, regardless of what macOS was storing internally.</p>

        <p>Copy the picture, open <a href="https://pastedownloadapp.com/">pastedownloadapp.com</a>, press <strong>Cmd+V</strong>, and download it as PNG, JPEG, or WebP. The full walkthrough is in <a href="/blog/save-clipboard-image-without-software/">saving clipboard images without software</a>.</p>

        <figure class="my-6 overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-hairline bg-canvas-soft">
