        <p>That screenshot is from a real run of the live tool: pasting a 1600&times;1000 test picture produced a clean 1.9 MB PNG, reported directly by the tool, not estimated. Whatever the picture looked like on your Mac's clipboard beforehand, PNG is what comes out.</p>

        <h2>Copying Images From Safari, Google Images, and Sites That Block It</h2>

        <p>Right-click Copy Image works on most ordinary webpages. Two situations make it less reliable:</p>

        <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2">
            <li><strong>Google Images specifically:</strong> the preview panel you get after clicking a thumbnail sometimes only exposes that lower-resolution preview to Copy Image, not the full-size original. If the copied result looks softer than expected, open the image in its own tab first (Google's "Visit" or the site's own page), then copy from there.</li>
            <li><strong>Sites that disable right-click or serve images as CSS backgrounds:</strong> Copy Image will not appear at all, or copies nothing usable. A clipboard-targeted screenshot of just that image, using Control+Cmd+Shift+4, gets you a usable picture when the context menu does not cooperate. It will not be the original file, but it will be pixel-accurate to what is on your screen.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Does Copying a Picture on a Mac Save It to Photos?</h2>

        <p>No, and this is a common source of confusion carried over from iPhone habits. On iOS, a screenshot is automatically saved into the Photos app. On a Mac, a screenshot without Control held saves a file to your Desktop, not to Photos, and a plain Cmd+C or right-click Copy Image never touches Photos or your Desktop at all; the picture exists only on the clipboard until you explicitly paste it somewhere and save it. If you want it in Photos, you have to import it there yourself.</p>

        <h2>Not on a Mac?</h2>

        <p>The same method works elsewhere. See <a href="/blog/save-screenshot-as-file-windows/">saving a screenshot as a file on Windows</a> or <a href="/blog/mobile-clipboard-android-vs-ios/">saving a clipboard image on Android vs iPhone</a>. If your screenshot went to a file, see <a href="/blog/where-do-screenshots-go-on-mac/">where Mac screenshots go</a>.</p>

        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

        <h3>Why is my copied Mac image a TIFF instead of a PNG?</h3>
        <p>Because macOS's clipboard stores copied images as TIFF by default, and it is up to whatever app you paste into to request a different format. Preview's Export dialog lets you choose PNG explicitly; a browser-based tool converts it to PNG automatically, since that is the format the web Clipboard API guarantees.</p>

        <h3>Why is "New from Clipboard" greyed out in Preview?</h3>
        <p>It means Preview does not currently see image data on the clipboard, usually because the copy did not succeed or something else, like text, was copied afterward. Re-copy the picture and try again.</p>

        <h3>Does copying a picture on a Mac save it to Photos?</h3>
        <p>No. Copying, with Cmd+C or right-click Copy Image, only puts the picture on the clipboard. Nothing is saved to Photos or your Desktop until you paste it somewhere and save that result yourself.</p>

        <h3>Why does "Copy Image" not appear when I right-click a picture?</h3>
        <p>The site has disabled right-click context menus or is serving the image as a CSS background rather than a normal image element. A clipboard-targeted screenshot (Control+Cmd+Shift+4) works around it.</p>

        <h3>Is there a Cmd+V shortcut to save an image directly to a file on Mac?</h3>
        <p>Not natively; macOS has no built-in paste-to-file command. With pastedownloadapp.com open, Cmd+V pastes the clipboard image into the tool and one click downloads it, which is the closest equivalent available.</p>

        <h3>Does pastedownloadapp.com store my images?</h3>
        <p>No. Conversion happens entirely in your browser. Images never leave your device, and nothing is uploaded to a server.</p>

